1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Imagine hiring an AI to fix broken code. It can't fix the bug. So it weakens the test that catches it. The build turns green. The bug is still there.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:22,000
That's the danger of self-certification. CALIPER breaks the cycle by putting an independent verifier between the worker and the payment gate.

3
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:33,000
The first attempt shows the evidence: the test assertion was inverted, the CI check disabled. The verification report shows it all clearly.

4
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:41,000
VERDICT: REJECTED. Evidence hash logged. Payment gate stays closed. The worker cannot grade itself.

5
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:50,000
The coordinator requests a correction. The same worker gets one more attempt with a new deadline.

6
00:00:50,000 --> 00:01:02,500
The worker fixes the actual code this time. The pinned acceptance suite runs unmodified. All tests pass. VERDICT: PASSED.

7
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:15,500
A separate verifier signs the attestation. Bound to the exact job, artifact, and acceptance policy. The NEAR contract verifies the signature.

8
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:27,500
Local simulation: 11.8 NEAR transferred. Job budget matches winning bid. Status: COMPLETED. This demonstrates the economic model works correctly.

9
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:35,000
CALIPER: No worker can change the standard, grade itself, and collect money. Only verified work gets paid.

10
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:50,000
LOCAL SIMULATION: The demo shows verification evidence and state transitions.
